EXT. FUNERAL HOME PARKING LOT - FOOD TRUCK - DAY

A tamale truck parked between hearses. Mourners in black stand in line holding paper plates.

Six Castellanos-Whitfields. One truck.

ABUELA FLORENTINA, eighties, in a black lace mantilla, orders for everyone.

Her son, ARTURO, fifties, pays. Her daughter-in-law, KATHLEEN, fifties, wipes her eyes with a napkin.

Their daughters, IMANI, twenties, and DAHLIA, teens, argue over hot sauce. Their cousin, BASIL, thirties, eats from two plates.

FLORENTINA
Arturo, she wants pork. Give the girl pork.

ARTURO
Mama, she's vegetarian.

IMANI ^
I'm not vegetarian!

DAHLIA
Imani, you've been a vegetarian since June.

IMANI
Dahlia, mind your own plate!

KATHLEEN
Girls. Please. It's your grandfather's day.

BASIL
(mouth full)
He'd have loved this.

FLORENTINA
He hated tamales.

BASIL
He'd have loved the fight.

A silence. Then, one by one, they start to laugh.
